
The argv vector dump will respect UTF-8 characters 
just fine if you can put them into your terminal.

Thus : 

See https://www.unicode.org/charts/PDF/U20A0.pdf for the unicode
data points for currency symbols.

You will see the euro currency is 0x20AC --> € 

Lets expand that into binary and then encode as 
3 bytes of UTF-8 thus :

   0x20AC -->  0010 0000 1010 1100 binary 
               
               1110 xxxx 10xx xxxx 10xx xxxx  <-- 16bits encoded in three bytes 
 
                    0010   00 0010   10 1100  <-- actual data bits 
               1110 0010 1000 0010 1010 1100  <-- Euro currency 0x20AC codepoint in UTF-8 byte sequence 
 
                  E    2    8    2    A    C  -->   e2 82 ac  

Therefore the three bytes for a single euro currency symbol will
be e2 82 ac and we can demonstrate this :

 
hydra$ ./argv_dump Berenice\ spends\ 5€\ pour\ un\ caffe
-------------------------------------------------------------------
                 system name = FreeBSD
                   node name = hydra
                     release = 14.0-CURRENT
                     version = FreeBSD 14.0-CURRENT #0 main-n254876-b85b9c88eb0: Mon Apr 18 00:47:44 GMT 2022     root@hydra:/usr/obj/usr/src/amd64.amd64/sys/GENERIC
                     machine = amd64
                   page size = 4096
               system memory = 14690115584
                             = 14345816 kB
                             = 14009 MB
                 avail pages = 3586454
                avail memory = 0
         clock ticks per sec = 128
             threads support = 200112
               POSIX Version = 200112
                      endian = little endian
       sizeof(unsigned long) = 8
  sizeof(unsigned long long) = 8
                 sizeof(int) = 4
               sizeof(void*) = 8
           fp rounding mode is FE_TONEAREST
           system uptime    7 days    0 hour    2 mins   53 secs
-------------------------------------------------------------------
argc = 2
&argc = 0x820a9fca8
argv = 0x820a9fd10
11 chars for argv[ 0] = "./argv_dump"
34 chars for argv[ 1] = "Berenice spends 5€ pour un caffe"

hex dump of argv
----------------
2e 2f 61 72 67 76 5f 64 75 6d 70 00 42 65 72 65 6e 69 63 65 20 73 70 65 6e 64 73 20 35 e2 82 ac 20 70 6f 75 72 20 75 6e 20 63 61 66 66 65 00 
hydra$ 
 
Carefully look at the bytes and you will see 35 e2 82 ac 20 as a single string
which represents 5€ as a single ASCII 0x35 for the digit 5 and then the three
bytes for euro symbol.


Another example would be characters with accents : 

argc = 9
&argc = 0x820d31d98
argv = 0x820d31e00
11 chars for argv[ 0] = "./argv_dump"
 2 chars for argv[ 1] = "á"
 2 chars for argv[ 2] = "â"
 2 chars for argv[ 3] = "ã"
 2 chars for argv[ 4] = "ä"
 2 chars for argv[ 5] = "å"
 2 chars for argv[ 6] = "æ"
 2 chars for argv[ 7] = "ç"
 2 chars for argv[ 8] = "è"

hex dump of argv
----------------
2e 2f 61 72 67 76 5f 64 75 6d 70 00 c3 a1 00 c3 a2 00 c3 a3 00 c3 a4 00 c3 a5 00 c3 a6 00 c3 a7 00 c3 a8 00 
hydra$ 


